  <ferc:DisclosureOfStatusOfAnyMateriallyImportantLegalProceedingsTextBlock id="HH22FBAEA0509E80B804A4341B1670898A" contextRef="HH6F430EA68AFC29D1DD829B0DDD45FFF4">&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;n/a&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfStatusOfAnyMateriallyImportantLegalProceedingsTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfMateriallyImportantTransactionsOfTheRespondentNotDisclosedElsewhereTextBlock id="HH582A4E700260C05D782FA2D6F218758A" contextRef="HH6F430EA68AFC29D1DD829B0DDD45FFF4">&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;n/a&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfMateriallyImportantTransactionsOfTheRespondentNotDisclosedElsewhereTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfChangesInOfficersDirectorsMajorSecurityHoldersAndVotingPowersOfTheRespondentTextBlock id="HHC9E9219375AC2F82A8CDFAF00D88FECC" contextRef="HH6F430EA68AFC29D1DD829B0DDD45FFF4">&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif; font-size: medium; text-indent: -24px;"&gt;All of the membership interest in Kinetica Partners, LLC were purchased by Eckard Midstream, LLC in a transaction that closed on December 27, 2023.&amp;#160; The Chairman of the Board of Eckard Midstream LLC owned or controlled a minority membership interest in Kinetica Partners prior to his December 27&lt;/span&gt;&lt;sup style="font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif; text-indent: -24px;"&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif; font-size: medium; text-indent: -24px;"&gt;&amp;#160;purchase of the remaining members&amp;#8217; interests.&amp;#160; There were no changes to the ownership or control of Kinetica Deepwater Express, LLC.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfChangesInOfficersDirectorsMajorSecurityHoldersAndVotingPowersOfTheRespondentTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfSignificantEventThatRespondentParticipatesInACashManagementProgramsTextBlock id="HHD790CCB2EDB33AB49E3957CEAFD4E7D7" contextRef="HH6F430EA68AFC29D1DD829B0DDD45FFF4">&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;n/a&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfSignificantEventThatRespondentParticipatesInACashManagementProgramsTextBlock>
  <ferc:NotesToFinancialStatementsTextBlock id="HHFB845F6A13C048AB10E053139FD9FDD6" contextRef="HH6F430EA68AFC29D1DD829B0DDD45FFF4">&lt;div&gt;&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14pt; line-height: 21.4667px;"&gt;Note 1 Company Business&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;Kinetica Deepwater Express, LLC, a Delaware limited liability company, (The Company) is a wholly-owned, subsidiary of Kinetica Partners, LLC, a Texas limited liability company (The Parent). The Company provides natural gas transmission from producing fields located in federal waters of the Gulf of Mexico offshore Louisiana.&amp;#160; In addition, the Company provides associated liquids transportation and handling services.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14pt; line-height: 21.4667px;"&gt;Note 2 Significant Accounting Policies&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Basis of Presentation&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;The Company is regulated by the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission (FERC).The financial statements presented herein are prepared in accordance with the accounting regulations of the FERC, as set forth in the applicable Uniform System of Accounts and published accounting releases, which is a comprehensive basis of accounting separate from accounting principles generally accepted in the United States.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;The most significant differences between accounting principles generally accepted in the United States and the financial statements presented herein are the classification of certain accounts on the balance sheet, statement of income, and the statement of cash flows.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Use of Estimates&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;The preparation of our financial statements requires the use of estimates and assumptions that affect the reported amounts of assets and liabilities, revenue, expenses and our disclosures in these financial statements. While the company believes its estimates are appropriate, actual results can, and often do, differ from those estimates.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Revenue Recognition&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;We recognized revenue as service is provided. Customers are invoiced and the related revenue is recorded in the period transportation services on our pipeline are provided to customers.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Cash and Cash Equivalents&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;The Company considers cash and cash equivalents as cash on hand, cash in bank accounts, and highly liquid investments with original maturities of three months or less.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Trade Accounts Receivable&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;Trade accounts receivable are recorded at the invoiced amount and do not bear interest, except for those receivables subject to late charges. The Company maintains an allowance for doubtful accounts for estimated losses on accounts receivable due from shippers and operators if it is determined the Company will not collect all or part of the outstanding receivable balance. The Company regularly reviews its allowance for doubtful accounts and establishes or adjusts the allowance as necessary using the specific-identification method. Account balances are charged to the allowance after all means of collection have been exhausted and the potential for recovery is no longer considered probable. Accounts charged to the allowance in 2025 and 2024 were $0, respectively.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Material and Supplies&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;The Company&amp;#8217;s inventory consists of materials and supplies. The materials and supplies are valued at cost with cost determined using the average cost method.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Property, Plant and Equipment&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;Property, plant and equipment are stated at original cost of construction less accumulated depreciation. The Company capitalizes all construction-related direct labor and material costs. The costs of renewals and betterments that extend the useful life or increase the expected output of property, plant and equipment are also capitalized. The costs of repairs, replacements and major maintenance projects that do not extend the useful life or increase the expected output of property, plant and equipment, are expensed as incurred.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Depreciation&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;The Company uses the composite (group) method to depreciate regulated property, plant and equipment. Under this method, assets with similar useful lives and other characteristics are grouped and depreciated as one asset. The depreciation rate is applied to the total cost of the group until its net book value equals its salvage value. All asset groups are depreciated using depreciation rates approved in the Company&amp;#8217;s last rate proceeding. Currently, the Company&amp;#8217;s depreciation rates vary from 0.25% to 20% per year. Using these rates, the remaining depreciable life of these assets ranges from 2 to 64 years. Once the Group rate and the average service life are determined, they normally are not changed despite the addition and disposition of individual assets. This implicitly assumes that the service lives of new assets approximate those of individual assets they replace.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;When property, plant, and equipment are retired, the Company charges accumulated depreciation and amortization for the original cost of the assets in addition to the cost to remove, sell, or dispose of the assets, less their salvage value. The Company does not recognize a gain or loss unless an entire operating unit is sold or retired. The Company includes gains or losses on dispositions of operating units in income.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;The Company capitalizes a carrying cost on funds invested in the construction of long-lived assets. This carrying cost includes a return on the investment financed by equity allowance for funds used during construction (AFUDC). AFUDC is calculated based on the Company&amp;#8217;s average cost of equity and is reflected as an increase in the cost of the asset on the balance sheets.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Natural Gas Imbalances&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;Natural gas imbalances occur when the actual amount of natural gas delivered to or received from a pipeline system differs from the amount of natural gas scheduled to be delivered or received. These imbalances can be settled through cash outs (purchases or sales at the current month index price) or in-kind (with natural gas at some time in the future) subject to the terms of our tariff or agreement. We value these imbalances due to or from shippers and operators at the current market index price. Imbalances due from others are reported on the balance sheet as miscellaneous current and accrued assets. Imbalances owed to others are reported on the balance sheet as miscellaneous current and accrued liabilities. The Company classifies all imbalances as current as the Company expects to settle any imbalances within a year.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Asset Retirement Obligations&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;The Company accounts for asset retirement obligations pursuant to the provisions of Financial Accounting Standards Board (FASB) Accounting Standards Codification (ASC) 410-20, Asset Retirement Obligations. ASC 410-20 requires the Company to record the fair value of an asset retirement obligation as a liability in the period in which it incurs a legal obligation associated with the retirement of tangible long-lived assets that result from the acquisition, construction, development, and/or normal use of the assets. ASC 410-20 also requires the Company to record a corresponding asset that is depreciated over the life of the asset. Subsequent to the initial measurement of the asset retirement obligation, the obligation is to be adjusted at the end of each period to reflect the passage of time and changes in the estimated future cash flows underlying the obligation. The Company has determined it has legal obligations associated with its natural gas pipelines and related facilities. The obligations associated with its transmission facilities relate primarily to purging and sealing the pipelines if they are abandoned.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;The Company is also required to operate and maintain its natural gas pipeline system, and intends to do so as long as supply and demand for natural gas exists, which the Company expects for the foreseeable future. Therefore, the Company believes its natural gas pipelines have indeterminate lives and, accordingly, has recorded no asset retirement obligation as of December 31, 2025 and 2024. The Company continues to evaluate its asset retirement obligations and future developments that could impact the amounts it records.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Customer Deposits&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;The Company received certain customer deposits to perform certain capital maintenance projects on behalf of its customers. No revenue and expenses are recognized for the performance of these projects as the Company is only responsible for the facilitation of payments on behalf of its customers.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14pt; line-height: 21.4667px;"&gt;Note 3 Income Taxes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;The Company is organized as a Delaware limited liability company and is disregarded for federal income tax purposes. Income taxes associated with its operations are the responsibility of its members and would not generally be reflected in its financial statements.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14pt; line-height: 21.4667px;"&gt;Note 4 Related Party Transactions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;Balances in accounts receivable and accounts payable with Associated Companies include activities in the normal course of business. Direct charges, allocated salaries, overhead expenses paid by the parent company, Kinetica Partners, LLC (KPLLC), and cash receipts advanced from KDE are part of account receivables and /or payables to Associated Companies in KDE&amp;#8217;s financial statements.&amp;#160; Occasionally, KDE will have receipts that belong to Kinetica Midstream Energy, LLC (KME), and Kinetica Energy Express, LLC (KEE), affiliated companies. In the same manner KME, and KEE will have sale receipts that belong to KDE. These transactions create an intercompany receivable / (payable) from / to KDE. At December 31, 2025 and 2024, the company had accounts receivable / (payable) as follows (in 000&amp;#8217;s):&lt;/p&gt;
